1
00:00:02,115 --> 00:00:04,600
So now we solved a lot of problems here with

2
00:00:04,600 --> 00:00:06,363
different tools for different kinds of

3
00:00:06,363 --> 00:00:08,319
problems, and therefore hopefully you now

4
00:00:08,431 --> 00:00:10,980
have a good toolset for solving problems you

5
00:00:11,012 --> 00:00:12,983
might be facing in your programs.

6
00:00:13,657 --> 00:00:16,318
We're not 100% done though. There is one

7
00:00:16,382 --> 00:00:17,680
last thing what you want to do

8
00:00:18,225 --> 00:00:21,415
because I did also promise you an improved

9
00:00:21,415 --> 00:00:23,595
developer experience, and I was not just

10
00:00:23,595 --> 00:00:25,951
talking about finding and fixing errors when

11
00:00:26,064 --> 00:00:26,929
I referred to that.

12
00:00:27,546 --> 00:00:30,038
Instead, let's now build a basic server

13
00:00:30,038 --> 00:00:30,346
again

14
00:00:32,158 --> 00:00:33,705
where we import serve

15
00:00:36,327 --> 00:00:36,778
from

16
00:00:37,695 --> 00:00:44,453
https://deno.land/std/http/server.ts.

17
00:00:45,970 --> 00:00:46,358
And there,

18
00:00:47,038 --> 00:00:49,985
let's again use a specific version to make

19
00:00:50,034 --> 00:00:51,264
sure this will always work.

20
00:00:52,074 --> 00:00:53,466
And then we can create our server.

21
00:00:54,163 --> 00:00:57,679
By calling serve, passing in the port 3000,

22
00:00:57,695 --> 00:00:58,354
for example.

23
00:00:59,221 --> 00:01:01,164
And then you might remember that we can also

24
00:01:01,212 --> 00:01:03,508
loop through all the requests with this

25
00:01:03,733 --> 00:01:04,632
syntax here,

26
00:01:05,563 --> 00:01:08,180
and we can then use request.respond to send

27
00:01:08,213 --> 00:01:11,408
back a body where we say message from Deno.

28
00:01:11,745 --> 00:01:13,672
This is how we can create a simple server

29
00:01:13,672 --> 00:01:14,234
with Deno.

30
00:01:15,053 --> 00:01:17,092
Now you also saw that we can run this with

31
00:01:17,188 --> 00:01:19,901
deno run basic server, and And of course, we

32
00:01:19,901 --> 00:01:23,316
need to add the allow net permission here so

33
00:01:23,316 --> 00:01:24,759
that we can spin up this server

34
00:01:24,759 --> 00:01:25,481
successfully.

35
00:01:26,090 --> 00:01:27,677
Now this server is running

36
00:01:28,415 --> 00:01:30,740
and on localhost:3000, we can see our

37
00:01:30,740 --> 00:01:32,888
message. But there's one annoying thing

38
00:01:32,985 --> 00:01:33,562
about that.

39
00:01:34,283 --> 00:01:36,993
Whenever we change something, like for

40
00:01:36,993 --> 00:01:37,939
example, the message,

41
00:01:38,997 --> 00:01:42,396
we have to quit the server with Ctrl+C and

42
00:01:42,445 --> 00:01:43,006
restart it.

43
00:01:43,743 --> 00:01:46,084
This is not impossible to do, but if you're

44
00:01:46,084 --> 00:01:48,281
doing a lot of development, constantly

45
00:01:48,313 --> 00:01:51,090
having to quit and restart can get annoying.

46
00:01:51,587 --> 00:01:53,625
It would be nice if it would automatically

47
00:01:53,689 --> 00:01:55,759
restart whenever we change something in a

48
00:01:55,759 --> 00:01:56,353
file here.

49
00:01:57,380 --> 00:01:59,947
Thankfully, Deno has a tool for us. It's

50
00:01:59,947 --> 00:02:02,419
part of the third-party modules, which we

51
00:02:02,579 --> 00:02:04,617
haven't really used thus far, but we can

52
00:02:04,617 --> 00:02:06,527
have a look at one third-party module right

53
00:02:06,527 --> 00:02:08,886
now, and that module is called Denon.

54
00:02:09,511 --> 00:02:10,972
If you search for Denon here,

55
00:02:12,288 --> 00:02:14,149
and the scrolling would work correctly on

56
00:02:14,149 --> 00:02:14,598
this page,

57
00:02:15,292 --> 00:02:16,688
So thanks for that, I guess.

58
00:02:18,668 --> 00:02:20,843
You should be able to find this package

59
00:02:20,875 --> 00:02:21,119
here,

60
00:02:21,640 --> 00:02:21,769
Denon.

61
00:02:23,275 --> 00:02:25,120
Denon is a package,

62
00:02:26,108 --> 00:02:28,601
a module to be precise, to stick to the

63
00:02:28,634 --> 00:02:29,978
wording the Deno team uses,

64
00:02:30,540 --> 00:02:33,240
which in the end allows us to wrap this Deno

65
00:02:33,402 --> 00:02:36,021
run process with another process

66
00:02:36,733 --> 00:02:39,919
so that this Denon tool can watch our files

67
00:02:40,114 --> 00:02:43,148
and restart Deno run whenever one of our

68
00:02:43,148 --> 00:02:44,013
files changed.

69
00:02:44,590 --> 00:02:47,155
A similar tool exists for Node, there it's

70
00:02:47,155 --> 00:02:47,925
called nodemon.

71
00:02:48,534 --> 00:02:50,778
Well, and here we got denon for Deno.

72
00:02:52,061 --> 00:02:54,273
Now this is not a module which we're going

73
00:02:54,305 --> 00:02:56,437
to include into one of our source files

74
00:02:56,549 --> 00:02:59,114
though, because of course this should not

75
00:02:59,114 --> 00:03:01,695
run as part of our code. Instead, we want to

76
00:03:01,759 --> 00:03:04,228
run our code with that tool, so it's the

77
00:03:04,228 --> 00:03:06,761
other way around. But you can also install

78
00:03:06,889 --> 00:03:10,046
some of Deno's modules globally on your

79
00:03:10,094 --> 00:03:13,267
system. The vast majority are import modules

80
00:03:13,620 --> 00:03:16,344
like the server module here— the HTTP module

81
00:03:16,344 --> 00:03:17,513
in the server file, I mean.

82
00:03:18,090 --> 00:03:20,718
Most modules are like this. They expose

83
00:03:20,814 --> 00:03:22,689
features which you can use in your code.

84
00:03:23,314 --> 00:03:25,894
But you also occasionally have modules that

85
00:03:25,894 --> 00:03:26,807
expose features

86
00:03:27,544 --> 00:03:29,708
which you can execute in the command line

87
00:03:29,708 --> 00:03:31,454
and which you therefore can install as

88
00:03:31,486 --> 00:03:34,178
global tools. And Denon is one of those

89
00:03:34,290 --> 00:03:34,739
packages.

90
00:03:35,477 --> 00:03:37,530
Now you see the installation command down

91
00:03:37,658 --> 00:03:40,481
there, so you can just copy that into your

92
00:03:40,545 --> 00:03:41,908
command line, quit the server,

93
00:03:42,454 --> 00:03:43,609
and run this command.

94
00:03:44,635 --> 00:03:47,249
And this will install this Deno module as a

95
00:03:47,362 --> 00:03:49,591
global tool on our system.

96
00:03:50,425 --> 00:03:52,526
So if I hit enter here, this downloads this

97
00:03:52,574 --> 00:03:54,387
tool and all its dependencies

98
00:03:55,205 --> 00:03:57,274
and, well, sets it up globally.

99
00:03:58,653 --> 00:04:01,364
Now with Deno installed, we can run Deno as

100
00:04:01,412 --> 00:04:04,412
a global tool. So now not Deno which also is

101
00:04:04,444 --> 00:04:06,162
still available, but for this purpose here,

102
00:04:06,291 --> 00:04:08,201
denon, which is now also available,

103
00:04:08,779 --> 00:04:10,995
and then your script name. So the root

104
00:04:11,204 --> 00:04:13,291
script that starts your application, in my

105
00:04:13,436 --> 00:04:14,784
case basic_server.ts.

106
00:04:15,587 --> 00:04:17,177
Now if I hit enter, you'll see that I of

107
00:04:17,209 --> 00:04:18,590
course get a permission error

108
00:04:19,441 --> 00:04:21,287
because we didn't specify the network

109
00:04:21,287 --> 00:04:23,936
permissions. But you also see that it didn't

110
00:04:23,952 --> 00:04:26,585
go back to the command line. Instead, now we

111
00:04:26,585 --> 00:04:27,774
got a running process.

112
00:04:28,303 --> 00:04:29,941
I'll come back to that. For the moment,

113
00:04:29,957 --> 00:04:32,928
let's quit it though with Ctrl+C and add a

114
00:04:32,928 --> 00:04:33,859
configuration file.

115
00:04:34,630 --> 00:04:35,673
You can add a

116
00:04:36,540 --> 00:04:39,993
.denon or.denon.json or.denonrc or

117
00:04:40,314 --> 00:04:41,646
.denonrc.json file.

118
00:04:42,738 --> 00:04:44,360
It doesn't matter. I'll just go with

119
00:04:45,067 --> 00:04:45,420
.denon

120
00:04:46,271 --> 00:04:48,824
and add such a file here. And in there you

121
00:04:48,824 --> 00:04:50,301
should have some JSON code

122
00:04:50,911 --> 00:04:53,255
where you can set up the settings you see

123
00:04:53,336 --> 00:04:55,701
down there in this example. You can define

124
00:04:55,814 --> 00:04:57,819
which files should be watched, you can

125
00:04:57,852 --> 00:04:59,922
define which file extensions should be

126
00:05:00,019 --> 00:05:01,765
watched, and so on and so on.

127
00:05:02,541 --> 00:05:04,936
And for me, the Deno args matter because

128
00:05:05,033 --> 00:05:07,674
that allows us to, for example, specify

129
00:05:07,950 --> 00:05:08,987
extra permissions.

130
00:05:10,570 --> 00:05:12,865
So here I'll set up the Deno

131
00:05:13,577 --> 00:05:14,320
args key.

132
00:05:15,517 --> 00:05:18,573
In JSON files, you should wrap your keys

133
00:05:18,605 --> 00:05:20,943
between double quotes, and I'll actually add

134
00:05:21,042 --> 00:05:23,724
the.json extension here so that I get

135
00:05:23,724 --> 00:05:25,876
better autocompletion and support by my IDE.

136
00:05:26,695 --> 00:05:29,633
And denoArgs should be an array, an array of

137
00:05:29,665 --> 00:05:31,720
your arguments or your flags which you

138
00:05:31,736 --> 00:05:33,326
wanted to add to deno run

139
00:05:33,904 --> 00:05:35,895
as strings. So in this case, I'll add

140
00:05:36,489 --> 00:05:38,897
this allowNet string here.

141
00:05:39,941 --> 00:05:42,285
Now this configuration file, if named like

142
00:05:42,285 --> 00:05:44,597
this, will automatically be picked up by the

143
00:05:44,661 --> 00:05:47,101
deno command. So if I now run deno

144
00:05:47,246 --> 00:05:50,216
basic-server.ts again you now see we don't

145
00:05:50,216 --> 00:05:53,010
get that permissions error because it looks

146
00:05:53,010 --> 00:05:55,129
into this configuration file and takes this

147
00:05:55,178 --> 00:05:56,559
configuration into account.

148
00:05:57,201 --> 00:05:59,529
And now the nice thing is if we change this

149
00:05:59,529 --> 00:06:02,387
again and for example remove this and save

150
00:06:02,419 --> 00:06:02,612
this,

151
00:06:03,302 --> 00:06:05,598
you see it detected one change and

152
00:06:05,711 --> 00:06:07,509
re-executed our script here.

153
00:06:08,200 --> 00:06:09,869
And that's what I meant, now it

154
00:06:09,966 --> 00:06:12,374
automatically restarts that script and we

155
00:06:12,455 --> 00:06:13,948
don't have to do this manually.

156
00:06:14,702 --> 00:06:16,789
And if we now go back and reload, we

157
00:06:16,789 --> 00:06:18,778
therefore see this updated message.

158
00:06:20,078 --> 00:06:21,715
Now of course you can also look into all

159
00:06:21,844 --> 00:06:24,138
these other configuration options to really

160
00:06:24,170 --> 00:06:26,096
fine-tune this to your requirements,

161
00:06:26,658 --> 00:06:28,728
but for our purposes, this basic

162
00:06:28,744 --> 00:06:31,392
configuration here should be just fine. The

163
00:06:31,392 --> 00:06:33,959
most important thing to not forget is that

164
00:06:33,959 --> 00:06:35,837
you should add your permission flags here,

165
00:06:36,270 --> 00:06:38,661
otherwise your script will just not run.

166
00:06:39,480 --> 00:06:41,373
And with that, I would say we made a huge

167
00:06:41,662 --> 00:06:44,615
leap towards a more fun developer experience

168
00:06:44,792 --> 00:06:46,501
because we don't continuously have to

169
00:06:46,630 --> 00:06:48,548
restart this. Instead, now it's being

170
00:06:48,581 --> 00:06:49,500
restarted for us.
